    <statement><p>The figure below shows the velocity field for the Gulf Stream.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap17Sec4__Q11__pg-image-1.png" width="111%"/>
<p>(Note that vectors in the graph are shown as arrows, so that the
flow through the middle of the graph moves primarily left-to-right.)
The vectors are shown on the graph at intervals of 30 km (thus, the
first, very small, vector at the bottom-left of the figure is at the
point <m>(0,0)</m>, and the vector immediately to the right of that is
at <m>(30,0)</m>, etc.).</p>
<p>For each of the following tables of velocity vectors (velocities
given in cm/sec) give the coordinates of the point on the velocity
field corresponding to the center vector in the table.</p>
<p><alert>(a)</alert></p>
<tabular bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium" top="medium">
<row>
<cell><m>95\vec i+43\vec j</m></cell>
